RATIONALE: The graph-theoretic core is *correct* but largely tautological: connected-components = single-linkage, single-linkage chains (Jain & Dubes 1988; ESL §14.3.12), Jaccard-above-T is non-transitive, ergo chain-endpoints can be arbitrarily dissimilar. Complete-linkage avoiding chains is its textbook definition. The L0 number 0.724 is a function of how many drift-chains the synthetic generator injects at a (T=0.8, consec=0.82) operating point the harness itself chose — it measures what was constructed. The researcher honestly flags this (effect → 0 at T=0.9 and when drift breaks consec<T) and explicitly punts chain-prevalence-in-real-corpora to L1; that punt is the load-bearing assumption. The non-circular, anti-folklore contribution would require either (i) a closed-form for innocent-removal vs graph topology not already in the single-linkage literature, or (ii) real-corpus measurement showing the chain-prevalence assumption holds at T~0.8. L0 provides neither. Hence the *structural mechanism* is HELD-but-textbook and the *magnitude claim* is conditional on a real-corpus property not measured. Vote YELLOW: not red (claim is internally consistent, mitigation arm is clean, EXACT-edges control rules out the trivial LSH-FP explanation, and the synthetic demo is honestly bounded), not green (headline 0.724 is a synthetic artifact of injected chain density; novelty vs single-linkage-chaining literature is unestablished; the real load-bearing number is deferred). prompt-version: theory_skeptic_v001
